Ladybird sizes an audio element with controls from its controls in the
block axis, in flex layout as in block layout, and keeps the 300px
fallback width when its width is auto. That’s what Gecko does:
nsVideoFrame::GetIntrinsicSize() gives an audio element with controls
the 300x150 fallback, whose width it uses for the frame’s intrinsic
widths. But nsVideoFrame::ComputeSize() sends an audio frame through
nsContainerFrame::ComputeSize(), and nsVideoFrame::Reflow() takes the
controls’ block size as the frame’s. Blink and WebKit instead give the
element a fixed height in their UA stylesheets: Blink’s html.css sets
54px, and WebKit’s modern media controls set a 31px height and minimum
height on the host. None of the three gives it the 150px fallback
height. With an auto width, Chrome makes the element 0px wide, from its
0x0 natural size in LayoutImage::GetNaturalDimensions().
